<p>Apple&#8217;s thunder keeps rolling this week. First it was the <a href="https://sixcolors.com/link/2026/10/apple-announces-new-ipad-mini/">iPad mini</a>, and now it&#8217;s a set of relatively minor announcements that would have just gotten lost in the anticipated introduction of OLED touchscreen MacBook Pro models on Wednesday.</p>
<p>Apple updated the base-model MacBook Pro <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/10/apple-introduces-the-new-14-inch-macbook-pro-with-m6/">from an M5 chip to an M6</a>. This is the <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2025/10/m5-macbook-pro-review-the-ultimate-computer/">low-end model</a> that basically exists to snag buyers who don&#8217;t want to use a MacBook Air. (To be fair, it&#8217;s got a much better display and active cooling, too.)</p>
<p>But don&#8217;t be confused: This is <em>not</em> Apple&#8217;s big MacBook Pro announcement of the week. What&#8217;s been abundantly clear is that whatever Apple announces Wednesday—whether it&#8217;s a set of replacements for the higher-end <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/03/m5-pro-macbook-pro-review-fast-familiar-friend/">M5 Pro and M5 Max MacBook Pros</a>, or an even higher-priced tier of OLED models <em>above</em> the existing high-end—will not impact the design or price of the $1,999 base M5 (now M6) model.</p>
<p>Apple also <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/10/apple-unveils-new-imac-with-m6/">bumped the iMac to M6</a>, retaining the existing design and colors <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2021/05/24-inch-m1-imac-review/">introduced in the M1 model</a>. There are some minor technical enhancements here, most notably Wi-Fi 7 and Bluetooth 6, with 2.5Gb Ethernet standard. But Apple does not seem interested in altering the Apple silicon iteration of the iMac in any substantial way.</p>
<p>Finally, Apple <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/10/apple-creator-studio-adds-powerful-new-ai-features-and-even-more-creative-tools/">announced a slew of Creator Studio updates</a>, showing that the company is not going to stand still when it comes to advancing its <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/01/apple-launches-creator-studio-pro-app-collection/">new creative software suite</a>. There&#8217;s new content in Final Cut, improvements to music creation and an AI helper in Logic, generative fill in Pixelmator, and a bunch of AI features (leveraging both Apple&#8217;s own models and ones from OpenAI) sprinkled throughout the bundle.</p>
<p>Busy week already! I&#8217;ll be in L.A. Wednesday for whatever Apple&#8217;s announcing, and will be sure to report back here.</p>
